WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:03.920
Ouch!

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:05.860
What do you think you’re doing?

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.600
The idea of talking trees has been capturing 
the human imagination for generations.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.200
Did you say something?

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.860
My bark is worse than my
bite.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:24.800
Okay, so maybe they don’t talk to us, 
but it turns out, trees can “talk” to each other.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.840
The trees are speaking to each other.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.880
But that does beg the question: 
What do trees have to talk about?

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.000
And can we learn to speak their language?

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:50.520
Underneath the soil, a vast and interconnected network of life links the trees through their root systems.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.820
But, they can’t talk to each other without help.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:02.340
The whole process starts with hub trees—the oldest 
and tallest trees in the forest.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.080
Hub trees have greater access to sunlight,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.900
and through the process of photosynthesis, end up producing more sugar than they actually need.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.780
Underground, fungi need sugar to survive.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.440
Most of their bodies are made up of 
a mass of threads called “mycelium.”

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.580
They grow within the root system of trees
 to absorb the excess sugar.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.160
In return, the mycelium provides the tree with the nutrients it needs from the soil.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:42.680
This symbiotic relationship is known as mycorrhiza, which stems from the Greek words for fungus and root.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.480
These tree-fungi relationships connect
the trees in the forest together,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:56.660
forming an underground communication network to exchange water and nutrients,

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:01.380
to nurture their seedlings,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.580
and even send warning signals when under threat.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.600
So, how many trees are really talking to each other?

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.900
To get a better picture of these forest relationships,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:25.860
a team of researchers used DNA analysis to map a fungal network in a patch of Canadian forest.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:35.700
Remarkably, they found that one tree was connected to 47 other trees!

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.460
Their models also showed that when hub trees were removed,

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:45.080
it would cause more connections to be lost 
than if trees were simply removed randomly.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.440
Studying these kinds of underground exchanges

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:56.900
will play a vital role in creating stronger, more resilient forests for the future.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.720
So, even though we might not be able to “talk” to trees,

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.920
at least we can still keep trying to understand their language.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.620
Who knows what they might say?

